\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Glioblastoma, IDH-wildtype multi parametric imaging characteristics. (A) Axial CT without contrast: ill-defined hypodensity lesion centered in the left superior temporal gyrus; (B) axial DWI: no associated reduced diffusion; (C) axial T1 pre contrast: hypointense mass; (D) coronal T2: heterogeneous mass with hypointense rim and prominent central necrosis (yellow arrow); (E) axial T2: heterogeneous mass with hypointense rim and prominent central necrosis (yellow arrow); (F) axial SWI: prominent blood products within the mass (black arrow); (G) axial ASL perfusion: marked hyperperfusion (black arrows) within the rim-enhancing component of the mass; (H) axial T1 post contrast: heterogeneous mass with thick rim enhancement (yellow arrows) and prominent central necrosis; (I) coronal T1 post contrast: heterogeneous mass with thick rim enhancement and prominent central necrosis; (J) sagittal T1 post contrast: heterogeneous mass with thick rim enhancement and prominent central necrosis. Abbreviations: CT, computed tomography; DWI, diffusion weighted imaging; SWI, susceptibility weighted imaging; ASL, arterial spin labeling; MRS, magnetic resonance spectroscopy. Adapted from \cite {carrete2022advanced}}}{2}{figure.caption.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Pseudoprogression in Glioblastoma. (A) Immediate pre Radiotherapy: axial T1 post contrast images show rim enhancing and centrally necrotic left frontoparietal Glioblastoma; (B) Eight week follow-up: immediate post Radiotherapy axial T1 post contrast images show marked increase in enhancement and necrosis; (C) dynamic susceptibility weighted contrast enhanced perfusion MRI shows mild increase in blood volume along the posterior rim (black arrows), while single voxel proton spectroscopy targeted to the posterior component shows a markedly increased lipid peak suggesting tissue necrosis; (D) Three month follow up: axial T1 contrast images show marked decrease in enhancement and necrosis of the treated Glioblastoma. Adapted from \cite {carrete2022advanced}.}}{4}{figure.caption.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Longitudinal response assessment according to the Response Assessment in Neuro-Oncology (RANO) 2.0 criteria. In newly diagnosed Glioblastoma, the post Radiotherapy MRI is used as the baseline for subsequent response assessment. Because pseudoprogression is frequent during the first 12 weeks after completion of radiotherapy or concurrent chemoradiotherapy, apparent progression during this period requires confirmation by repeat MRI or histopathological evidence of unequivocal recurrent tumor before progressive disease is declared. This temporal framework highlights the importance of incorporating scan timing, treatment exposure, and serial imaging trajectories into computational models of tumor dynamics. Adapted from \cite {sakata2024rano}.}}{6}{figure.caption.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Molecular heterogeneity in Glioblastoma. Molecular alterations, including \textit {IDH} mutation status, \textit {MGMT} promoter Methylation, \textit {TERT} promoter mutation, \textit {EGFR} amplification, \textit {PTEN} alteration, \textit {CDKN2A/B} deletion, and \textit {TP53} alteration, influence prognosis, treatment sensitivity, and expected tumor behavior. \textit {IDH}-mutant Gliomas generally have a more favorable prognosis than \textit {IDH}-wildtype tumors, whereas \textit {MGMT} promoter methylation is associated with greater temozolomide sensitivity and improved survival. These molecular features provide important biological context for interpreting apparently similar MRI phenotypes and for personalizing computational models of tumor growth and treatment response. Adapted from \cite {zhu2024molecular}.}}{7}{figure.caption.5}%
